      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm doing a paper for my history class. how would modern historians look at how effective the UN actually was at stopping big wars in the 21st century? like was it a success or a failure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Most modern historians view the UN as a \"partial failure\" in preventing major 21st-century imperialist wars, as it failed to stop the 2003 Iraq invasion and the 2021 collapse of the Taliban; however, the organization largely succeeded in containing major human rights atrocities and ethnic conflicts in the 1990s and 2010s through \"strong peacekeeping.\"\n\nThe consensus is that the UN has not been the \"strong peacekeeper\" many hoped for, as it often prioritizes political compromise over stopping war itself; instead of being a \"moral arbiter\" that unilaterally enforces peace, it relies heavily on the \"good will\" of the 10 most powerful permanent members of the Security Council.\n\nUltimately, critics argue the UN acts more as a \"mediator in the chaos of war\" rather than a true \"war stopper,\" especially when the P5 are in disagreement; this reality has left the UN as a \"failing institution\" capable of only \"controlling the worst effects of conflict\" rather than ending it.",
